{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_gznbtnvxjodg6fuqx6oeo4o4zreulysc4ecespha4ujbu2r7nkjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases passed, and one **real divergence was found and fixed**.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single translation unit: one exported function `hdr_compare` (MPEG audio frame-header comparison) plus a `static` helper `hdr_valid`.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` on both `.so`s. C's entire ABI is `{hdr_compare}`; Rust exports it with the identical name. `hdr_valid` is `static` and correctly *not* exported by either. 0 unresolved non-libc symbols. Nothing was left untranslated or stubbed.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 25 rows, mechanically grepped from the C. The C has no error enum/`errno`/null check, so its rejection surface is the 9 short-circuiting sub-conditions, plus null/truncated/bogus-pointer rows and the reserved bit-field (\"out-of-range enum\") encodings.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 36 rows over 12 axes the C actually branches on (sync class, layer, bitrate, sample-rate, CRC bit, padding bits, the three `h1`↔`h2` relations, free-format agreement, pointer shape).\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n66 tests, all passing across **debug × release × {default, --no-default-features}** (`./verify.sh`, plus `HDR_STRIDE=1` to force every row full-size in debug). `Cargo.toml` declares no features, so that is the complete combination set; the script enumerates the power set mechanically anyway.\n\nThe decisive result is row **C36**: an exhaustive sweep of **all 2^40 = 1,099,511,627,776** combinations of the five bytes the C actually reads. Zero divergences; 255 of 256 sync-byte values yield 0 matches and `0xFF` yields exactly 283,584 — matching the independently-measured valid cross-product. This is *total* equivalence, not sampled equivalence.\n\n## The bug\n\nRelease-profile tests all passed. The **debug** profile exposed:\n\n```\nDIVERGENCE [h2=NULL]: C = Signal(11), Rust = Signal(6)\n```\n\nWith `-C debug-assertions=on` (default for `dev`/`test`), `rustc` emits a null-pointer precondition check before every raw `*ptr` deref. It panics; a panic escaping `extern \"C\"` aborts → **SIGABRT**. The C has no check — the load faults → **SIGSEGV**. Fixed by routing all loads through `core::ptr::read` (same single `movb`, no instrumentation), so behavior on invalid pointers now matches in every profile.\n\nTwo things made this findable, and both are now permanent guards:\n- Forked crash probes assert the **same signal**, not merely \"both failed\".\n- The harness had silently fallen back to the release `.so` during a debug run, hiding the bug on the first pass; `rust_so_path` is now **profile-strict**, and `rust_so_has_no_null_pointer_precondition_check` fails if a raw deref is reintroduced.\n\nBeyond return values, the suite also pins the C's memory-access contract: `h1[0]` never read, `h1` untouched when `hdr_valid(h2)` is false, no reads past index 2 (page-guarded buffers), and the exact byte-level lazy-read order (E21–E23). `c_src/` was not modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":489,"cache_read_input_tokens":180758,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":489,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"973cfeba-1468-4daa-aca7-a6c582b2005a","uuid":"de3d5c11-c309-4f7b-ab16-62d6fcf19d60","timestamp":"2026-08-26T22:51:12.329Z"}
