he C has no error enum/`errno`/null check, so its rejection surface is the 9 short-circuiting sub-conditions, plus null/truncated/bogus-pointer rows and the reserved bit-field (\"out-of-range enum\") encodings.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 36 rows over 12 axes the C actually branches on (sync class, layer, bitrate, sample-rate, CRC bit, padding bits, the three `h1`↔`h2` relations, free-format agreement, pointer shape).\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n66 tests, all passing across **debug × release × {default, --no-default-features}** (`./verify.sh`, plus `HDR_STRIDE=1` to force every row full-size in debug). `Cargo.toml` declares no features, so that is the complete combination set; the script enumerates the power set mechanically anyway.\n\nThe decisive result is row **C36**: an exhaustive sweep of **all 2^40 = 1,099,511,627,776** combinations of the five bytes the C actually reads. Zero divergences; 255 of 256 sync-byte values yield 0 matches and `0xFF` yields exactly 283,584 — matching the independently-measured valid cross-product. This is *total* equivalence, not sampled equivalence.\n\n## The bug\n\nRelease-profile tests all passed. The **debug** profile exposed:\n\n```\nDIVERGENCE [h2=NULL]: C = Signal(11), Rust = Signal(6)\n```\n\nWith `-C debug-assertions=on` (default for `dev`/`test`), `rustc` emits a null-pointer precondition check before every raw `*ptr` deref. It panics; a panic escaping `extern \"C\"` aborts → **SIGABRT**. The C has no check — the load faults → **SIGSEGV**. Fixed by routing all loads through `core::ptr::read` (same single `movb`, no instrumentation), so behavior on invalid pointers now matches in every profile.\n\nTwo things made this findable, and both are now permanent guards:\n- Forked crash probes assert the **same signal**, not merely \"both failed\".\n- The harness had silently fallen back to the release `.so` during a debug run, hiding the bug on the first pass; `rust_so_path` is now **profile-strict**, and `rust_so_has_no_null_pointer_precondition_check` fails if a raw deref is reintroduced.\n\nBeyond return values, the suite also pins the C's memory-access contract: `h1[0]` never read, `h1` untouched when `hdr_valid(h2)` is false, no reads past index 2 (page-guarded buffers), and the exact byte-level lazy-read order (E21–E23). `c_src/` was not modified.","ttft_ms":4661,"ttft_stream_ms":4513,"time_to_request_ms":45,"type":"result","duration_ms":3465732,"uuid":"3e14c68d-e1eb-4afd-a8b2-f50d54dd64b6","queued_turn_count":0}
